tel 1.0

base frontier
import soundness

info   explains a missing given as a tree of the candidates tried and the classpath givens that would match
detail
    _Frontier_ replaces the compiler's terse _no given instance of type `T` was found_ with an
    explanation of the search: which candidates were tried, which of their own contextual
    parameters could not be found, and which givens elsewhere on the classpath — not imported,
    or in a choice package — would supply the missing value, with the import that names each.

    ```text
    contextual value not found

      ■ resolving DecimalConverter
      └─ ▸ propose decimalConverters.javaDecimalConverter
    ```

    It works through a single catch-all given, `explainMissingContext`, of type `[any] => any`,
    which is a candidate for every search. When it is tried, it re-runs the search itself and
    either finds that the search succeeds without it, in which case it fails and lets the
    compiler choose the instance with its type inference intact, or builds a `Diagnostic` tree
    of what was missing and aborts with that tree rendered as the message. The catch-all never
    contributes a term to a program: it is purely diagnostic.

    It relies on the annotation `@scala.annotation.internal.diagnostic`, which exists in the
    Proscala compiler that Soundness builds with. A given carrying it which aborts during an
    implicit search fails as an ordinary candidate would, so `summonFrom`, default `using`
    arguments and `NotGiven` behave as they do without it; but the compiler keeps its errors as
    a `DiagnosticFailure`, and if the whole search fails, that message is reported verbatim in
    place of the compiler's own (and of any `@implicitNotFound`). Under a compiler without the
    annotation the catch-all is not usable.

    The catch-all is brought into scope in either of two ways:

    - `import soundness.*` brings in `soundness.explainMissingContext`, an old-style `implicit
      def` rather than a `given` precisely so that a wildcard import includes it;
    - `import frontier.context.explainMissingContext` brings in the `given` by name, for code
      which does not import the umbrella.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    summon[Char is Concatenable]   // does not compile
    ```

    In a chain of `using` clauses, the explanation shown is the one for the deepest missing
    value — the innermost cause — since the catch-all expands there first. An explanation is
    bounded: a chain of candidates deeper than six, or more than 256 implicit searches in one
    explanation, is reported as missing rather than followed, and the rendered tree is cut at
    100 lines.

    The library is the single source set `frontier.core`. `Diagnostic` is deliberately not
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where it would clash with `harlequin.Diagnostic`:
    reach it as `frontier.Diagnostic`.

refer frontier.context.explainMissingContext
refer frontier.Diagnostic

entry .context   the choice package holding the catch-all given `explainMissingContext`, imported by name
  detail
      A package whose single given is imported by name, since `import frontier.context.*`
      does not import givens. Code which imports the `soundness` umbrella does not need it:
      `import soundness.*` brings in the equivalent `soundness.explainMissingContext`.
  refer soundness.explainMissingContext
  entry .explainMissingContext   explains a failed implicit search as a tree of candidates and classpath proposals
      A `transparent inline given` of type `[any] => any`, marked
      `@scala.annotation.internal.diagnostic`, so it is a candidate in every implicit search.
      When tried, it repeats the search without itself: if the search succeeds, the catch-all
      fails, so that the compiler selects the instance itself with its type variables still
      open; if the search fails, the catch-all aborts with a rendered `Diagnostic` tree, which
      the compiler reports as the error if no other candidate succeeds. For a target
      `NotGiven[X]` it succeeds trivially, so that the compiler's per-candidate negation turns
      it into a failure and `NotGiven`'s own instances decide, and `NotGiven[X]` holds exactly
      when there is no `X`.

      ```scala
      import frontier.context.explainMissingContext

      trait A
      trait B
      given mkA(using B): A = new A {}

      summon[A]   // does not compile
      ```

      The message reports the missing `B`, the innermost cause. A proposal for a polymorphic
      given names its type-parameter bindings (`textual = Char`), and a `NotGiven[X]`
      requirement of a candidate is shown as _prohibits X_, with the given that violates it.

entry .Diagnostic   a pre-rendered tree describing a failed implicit search, and its renderer
  detail
      A format-neutral model of an explanation: each node has a kind, a `#name` already
      rendered as `Text`, an optional `#label` and its `#children`. Because the names are
      already rendered, the renderer has no compiler dependency, and the same model can be
      built by any macro which needs to explain a failure — Wisteria's generic derivation
      attaches a field or variant name to each node as its label.

      The enum's six cases are its node kinds, each taking a name, an optional label and a
      list of children:

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Rendered as
      column  Node
      row  `Resolving`  _■ resolving T_  the root: the type being resolved
      row  `Candidate`  _▪ candidate g_  a given in scope which was tried
      row  `Requires`  _✗ requires T_  a contextual parameter of a candidate which could not be found
      row  `Propose`  _▸ propose pkg.g_  a given on the classpath, not in scope, which would supply the value
      row  `Prohibits`  _⊘ prohibits X_  a `NotGiven[X]` requirement, unwrapped to `X`; its child is the `Found` that violates it, or it has no child and the label `absent` when it holds
      row  `Found`  _✓ found T_  a value which was found
      ```

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val tree = frontier.Diagnostic.Resolving
        ( t"Holder",
          Unset,
          List(frontier.Diagnostic.Requires(t"DecimalConverter", Unset, Nil)) )

      frontier.Diagnostic.render(tree)
      ```

      `Diagnostic` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where it would clash with
      `harlequin.Diagnostic`.
  refer frontier.context.explainMissingContext
  entry .render   renders a diagnostic tree as ANSI-coloured text, under a headline, to at most a limit of lines
      The root should be a `Resolving` node, which supplies the _■ resolving T_ line; any other
      node is wrapped in one. `headline` defaults to _contextual value not found_, and `limit`
      to 100: at most that many lines of the tree are written, followed by _… n more_ when
      lines were omitted. A non-leaf subtree identical to one already written is written as
      its first line alone, labelled _as above_, since an explanation in a large scope could
      otherwise run to thousands of lines.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val shared = frontier.Diagnostic.Requires(t"Shared", Unset, Nil)
      val first = frontier.Diagnostic.Candidate(t"First", Unset, List(shared))

      frontier.Diagnostic.render(frontier.Diagnostic.Resolving(t"Root", Unset, List(first)))
      ```
  entry .treeStyle   the box-drawing style a diagnostic tree is drawn in
  entry .expandable   makes a `Diagnostic` expandable into its children, for drawing as a tree
  entry #name   the rendered name of the node's type or given
  entry #label   an optional annotation shown after the name, such as a field name or `absent`
  entry #children   the node's child nodes
  entry #rebuild   the same kind of node with the same name, but a new label and children

entry .internal   the macro behind `explainMissingContext`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Every step from the catch-all to its macro must be `transparent
      inline`: only transparent inline calls are expanded during typing, so a non-transparent
      step would leave the macro unexpanded while the catch-all is tried, and the search would
      spuriously succeed.
  entry .explanation   the transparent inline entry point of the explaining macro
  entry .explain   the macro which re-runs a search and builds and renders its `Diagnostic`
  entry .NoCandidates   matches an implicit search failure which had no candidates, giving its explanation
    detail
        An extractor over `quotes.reflect.ImplicitSearchFailure`, used by the macro.
    entry .unapply   the explanation of a search failure with no candidates, if it is one
